WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.196
The renal system matters to the respiratory therapist in three ways.

2
00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:15.744
First, urine output is a window on perfusion and kidney function, and positive pressure ventilation can shrink it.

3
00:00:15.744 --> 00:00:29.816
Second, diabetic ketoacidosis arrives on the ventilator as a Kussmaul breathing patient with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis you must not treat with the ventilator.

4
00:00:29.816 --> 00:00:39.951
Third, the kidney is the slow partner in acid base balance: lungs handle CO2, kidneys handle fixed acids and bicarbonate.

5
00:00:39.951 --> 00:00:46.400
We also walk through renal failure in the ventilated patient and the four Rs.

6
00:00:47.800 --> 00:01:01.622
The kidneys filter nearly two hundred liters a day, but normal urine output is only one to two liters per day because almost all of the filtrate is reabsorbed.

7
00:01:01.622 --> 00:01:17.530
In hourly terms, the diuretics deck gives normal thirty to sixty milliliters per hour, oliguria under thirty, anuria under fifty milliliters per day, and polyuria over sixty per hour.

8
00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.094
Hess uses one milliliter per kilogram per hour as its reference.

9
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:35.525
The RCP 105 critical care decks define oliguria more strictly as under twenty per hour, or under four hundred milliliters in twenty four hours.

10
00:01:35.525 --> 00:01:45.000
Low urine output is a bad sign and points to shock, hypotension, poor cardiac output, or acute kidney injury.

11
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:56.938
Positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure, which drops venous return and cardiac output.

12
00:01:56.938 --> 00:02:01.810
Lower cardiac output means lower renal perfusion.

13
00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:14.436
The body senses the apparent volume loss and releases more antidiuretic hormone while releasing less atrial natriuretic factor.

14
00:02:14.436 --> 00:02:26.466
The net result is reduced urine output and fluid retention, which can push a borderline patient into acute kidney injury.

15
00:02:26.466 --> 00:02:37.103
Oliguria at the bedside typically indicates fluid deficiency, poor renal perfusion, or poor cardiac output.

16
00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:46.250
During IV potassium replacement, keep urine output above forty to fifty per hour to be safe.

17
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:59.498
In type one diabetes, the insulin producing cells in the pancreas are destroyed, so there is little or no insulin.

18
00:02:59.498 --> 00:03:09.149
Without insulin, glucose cannot enter cells and piles up in the blood, causing hyperglycemia.

19
00:03:09.149 --> 00:03:18.697
Excess glucose spills into the urine, drags water with it, and produces an osmotic diuresis.

20
00:03:18.697 --> 00:03:29.490
The patient is thirsty, called polydipsia, and urinates heavily, called polyuria, which dehydrates them.

21
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:38.934
Cells that cannot use glucose start burning fat, producing ketone bodies, which are acidic.

22
00:03:38.934 --> 00:03:47.547
The result is a metabolic acidosis driven by fixed acids the lungs cannot blow off.

23
00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:53.567
Severe hyperglycemia can also produce a hyperosmolar coma.

24
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:13.097
The classic case: a seventeen year old in semi coma, with polydipsia, polyuria, weakness, rapid weight loss, Kussmaul breathing at twenty four, heart rate one hundred five, blood pressure one fifteen over seventy.

25
00:04:13.097 --> 00:04:17.005
Urine ketones four, glucose six hundred fifty.

26
00:04:17.005 --> 00:04:31.194
Treatment is immediate IV normal saline for the dehydration and IV insulin to stop ketone production, then transition to subcutaneous insulin within forty eight hours.

27
00:04:31.194 --> 00:04:38.671
Replace electrolytes, especially potassium, because insulin drives potassium into cells.

28
00:04:38.671 --> 00:04:51.075
Bicarbonate therapy is reserved for pH under seven point one and controversial, because it can cause paradoxical CNS acidosis and volume overload.

29
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:59.306
The anion gap equals sodium minus the sum of chloride plus bicarbonate.

30
00:04:59.306 --> 00:05:05.057
The RCP 103 deck gives the normal range as nine to fourteen.

31
00:05:05.057 --> 00:05:16.272
An anion gap above fourteen signals a high gap acidosis, meaning fixed acids like ketones or lactate are flooding in.

32
00:05:16.272 --> 00:05:23.652
Their unmeasured anions widen the gap while bicarbonate is used up buffering.

33
00:05:23.652 --> 00:05:35.250
A normal gap acidosis is a bicarbonate loss, as in severe diarrhea, where chloride rises to replace the lost bicarbonate.

34
00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.839
DKA is a high gap acidosis.

35
00:05:37.839 --> 00:05:46.657
Classic example from Hess case two: sodium one forty, chloride one oh five, bicarbonate ten.

36
00:05:46.657 --> 00:05:51.929
Gap equals one forty minus one fifteen, so twenty five.

37
00:05:51.929 --> 00:05:53.175
That is high.

38
00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:11.070
The deck's DKA ABG: pH six point nine eight, PaCO2 twenty four, bicarbonate six, PaO2 ninety eight, SaO2 ninety seven percent.

39
00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:21.005
The read is severe metabolic acidosis with partial respiratory compensation.

40
00:06:21.005 --> 00:06:32.769
The very low PaCO2 is Kussmaul breathing, the body trying to blow off CO2 to raise the pH.

41
00:06:32.769 --> 00:06:41.789
Compensation is partial because the metabolic derangement is extreme.

42
00:06:41.789 --> 00:06:51.854
Oxygenation is fine; DKA is an acid base problem, not an oxygenation problem.

43
00:06:51.854 --> 00:07:11.200
The rule of thumb for metabolic acidosis compensation: PaCO2 falls about one point two for every one bicarbonate falls, with full response in hours.

44
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:22.938
Kussmaul breathing is deep and rapid, air hungry, with large tidal volumes and a regular rhythm with no apnea.

45
00:07:22.938 --> 00:07:33.464
It appears in severe metabolic acidosis, classically DKA, and is a compensatory maneuver, not a primary problem.

46
00:07:33.464 --> 00:07:46.715
The ventilator trap: if a ventilated patient has a low PaCO2 because they are compensating for metabolic acidosis, do not reduce ventilation.

47
00:07:46.715 --> 00:07:52.354
The patient needs that hyperventilation to protect their pH.

48
00:07:52.354 --> 00:08:01.188
The flip side is also true: do not raise ventilatory support to try to fix metabolic acidosis.

49
00:08:01.188 --> 00:08:02.692
Treat the cause.

50
00:08:02.692 --> 00:08:06.075
For DKA, that is fluids and insulin.

51
00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.594
Lungs control carbon dioxide, kidneys control bicarbonate.

52
00:08:12.594 --> 00:08:21.729
The lungs blow off carbonic acid as CO2, but they cannot excrete the other acids produced by metabolism.

53
00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:28.844
The kidneys handle those fixed acids: phosphoric, uric, lactic, and ketone acids.

54
00:08:28.844 --> 00:08:40.350
They manage pH by reabsorbing and regenerating bicarbonate, which neutralizes acids, and by secreting hydrogen ions into the urine.

55
00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:46.059
Only the renal system can regulate alkaline balance in the blood.

56
00:08:46.059 --> 00:08:54.404
In acidosis, the kidney holds bicarbonate and dumps hydrogen; in alkalosis it does the reverse.

57
00:08:54.404 --> 00:09:03.275
The kidney handles about two percent of the daily acid load; the lungs handle the other ninety eight.

58
00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:10.774
Each kidney has about a million nephrons, and each nephron does three things.

59
00:09:10.774 --> 00:09:18.741
One, glomerular filtration pushes fluid and solutes across the glomerulus under hydrostatic pressure.

60
00:09:18.741 --> 00:09:28.916
The glomerular filtration rate in healthy adults is roughly one hundred twenty to one hundred twenty five milliliters per minute.

61
00:09:28.916 --> 00:09:40.275
Two, tubular reabsorption reclaims plasma starting in the proximal tubule, which recovers eighty five to ninety percent of filtered bicarbonate.

62
00:09:40.275 --> 00:09:50.293
Three, tubular secretion clears waste by moving hydrogen, potassium, ammonium, creatinine, and organic acids into the filtrate.

63
00:09:50.293 --> 00:10:03.150
The main urinary buffers are phosphate and ammonia; ammonia binds hydrogen to form ammonium and lets the kidney excrete acid without acidifying the urine too much.

64
00:10:04.567 --> 00:10:06.860
Renal compensation is slow.

65
00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:14.334
The deck says it takes three to five days to fully respond to a chronic change in PaCO2.

66
00:10:14.334 --> 00:10:29.622
The rule of thumb is bicarbonate changes about three point five to four milliequivalents per liter for every ten torr rise in PaCO2, and about four to five for every ten torr fall.

67
00:10:29.622 --> 00:10:38.540
Hess clarifies: the kidney starts responding as soon as PaCO2 moves, but full compensation can take days.

68
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:49.836
The distal tubules normally excrete about seventy millimoles of acid per day but can ramp up to seven hundred over roughly five days.

69
00:10:49.836 --> 00:10:55.017
Compare the lungs, which produce rapid pH changes in minutes.

70
00:10:56.433 --> 00:11:05.565
Positive pressure ventilation hurts the kidney by lowering cardiac output and renal perfusion.

71
00:11:05.565 --> 00:11:20.719
The kidneys receive about twenty two to twenty five percent of cardiac output, so a drop in volume or output can slide the patient into renal insufficiency.

72
00:11:20.719 --> 00:11:29.559
Kidney failure shows up as falling urine output with rising BUN, creatinine, and potassium.

73
00:11:29.559 --> 00:11:36.456
Azotemia means abnormally high nitrogen containing wastes in the blood.

74
00:11:36.456 --> 00:11:54.233
The clinical risk is that the kidney can no longer eliminate the daily acid load, so metabolic acidosis builds up and the patient compensates with tachypnea and Kussmaul respirations.

75
00:11:55.633 --> 00:11:57.317
Memorize the four Rs.

76
00:11:57.317 --> 00:12:00.925
Retention of waste raises BUN and creatinine.

77
00:12:00.925 --> 00:12:05.655
Retention of fluid produces peripheral and pulmonary edema.

78
00:12:05.655 --> 00:12:18.563
Retention of potassium produces hyperkalemia, which is the most immediately life threatening electrolyte problem in renal failure because it causes dysrhythmias.

79
00:12:18.563 --> 00:12:25.057
Retention of drugs produces toxicity, so dose adjust renally cleared medications.

80
00:12:25.057 --> 00:12:31.792
The respiratory therapist's hook is this: where does excess fluid eventually end up?

81
00:12:31.792 --> 00:12:32.593
The lungs.

82
00:12:32.593 --> 00:12:39.248
That is why renal failure patients come to CPAP, BiPAP, and mechanical ventilation.

83
00:12:39.248 --> 00:12:44.058
The treatment for severe renal failure acidosis is dialysis.

84
00:12:45.467 --> 00:12:49.876
A patient with known diabetes arrives obtunded.

85
00:12:49.876 --> 00:12:56.536
Labs: sodium one thirty eight, chloride one hundred, bicarbonate eight.

86
00:12:56.536 --> 00:12:58.600
Compute the anion gap.

87
00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:04.417
Then classify the metabolic picture as high gap or normal gap.

88
00:13:05.333 --> 00:13:11.333
[6-second practice pause]

89
00:13:11.833 --> 00:13:18.272
The anion gap is one thirty eight minus one oh eight, which equals thirty.

90
00:13:18.272 --> 00:13:26.451
That is well above the normal nine to fourteen band, so this is a high gap metabolic acidosis.

91
00:13:26.451 --> 00:13:30.975
In a diabetic patient with those numbers, think DKA.

92
00:13:30.975 --> 00:13:47.158
The next moves are check glucose and ketones, start normal saline and IV insulin, replace potassium carefully, and leave the hyperventilation alone because it is protective compensation.

93
00:13:48.567 --> 00:13:57.518
A ventilated patient has produced one hundred twenty milliliters of urine over the last six hours.

94
00:13:57.518 --> 00:14:03.272
By the diuretics deck threshold, does this qualify as oliguria?

95
00:14:03.272 --> 00:14:09.757
And by the RCP 105 critical care deck, does it still count as oliguria?

96
00:14:09.757 --> 00:14:11.767
Pause and answer both.

97
00:14:12.667 --> 00:14:18.667
[6-second practice pause]

98
00:14:19.167 --> 00:14:26.949
Hourly rate is one hundred twenty divided by six, equals twenty milliliters per hour.

99
00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:35.647
By the diuretics deck, oliguria is under thirty milliliters per hour, so yes, this is oliguria.

100
00:14:35.647 --> 00:14:48.007
By the stricter RCP 105 critical care definition of under twenty per hour, the patient is right at the edge, so it qualifies there too.

101
00:14:48.007 --> 00:14:50.204
Either way, this is low.

102
00:14:50.204 --> 00:15:00.917
Think perfusion, volume, cardiac output, and the ventilator's effect on venous return before reaching for a diuretic.

103
00:15:02.333 --> 00:15:03.181
To recap.

104
00:15:03.181 --> 00:15:17.216
Normal urine output is one to two liters per day or thirty to sixty milliliters per hour; oliguria is under thirty and anuria is under fifty per day.

105
00:15:17.216 --> 00:15:32.099
DKA is a high anion gap metabolic acidosis driven by ketone acids, presenting with Kussmaul breathing; treat with saline and insulin, not with the ventilator.

106
00:15:32.099 --> 00:15:40.765
The anion gap is sodium minus the sum of chloride plus bicarbonate; normal nine to fourteen.

107
00:15:40.765 --> 00:15:48.678
The kidney handles fixed acids and bicarbonate, but slowly, over three to five days.

108
00:15:48.678 --> 00:15:53.482
Positive pressure ventilation reduces urine output.

109
00:15:53.482 --> 00:16:00.358
Renal failure brings the four Rs, and hyperkalemia is the most dangerous.
